.TH FRDPCTL 1 "July 2026" "FreeRDP" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
frdpctl \- inspect and control FRDP managed sessions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B frdpctl status
.RI [ --socket " path" ]
.br
.B frdpctl list-sessions
.RI [ --socket " path" ]
.br
.B frdpctl kill-session
.I id
.RI [ --socket " path" ]
.br
.B frdpctl reload
.RI [ --socket " path" ]
.br
.B frdpctl set-session-limits
.I id
.BI --max-processes " n"
.BI --memory-max-mb " n"
.BI --cpu-quota-percent " n"
.RI [ --socket " path" ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B frdpctl
uses the local session-manager IPC endpoint to report service reachability,
list managed sessions, close a named session, or reload supported session
policy.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B status
Report session-manager reachability and lifecycle-state counts.
.TP
.B list-sessions
List session identifiers, users, displays, states, and agent process IDs.
.TP
.BI kill-session " id"
Close the named managed session and its owned resources.
.TP
.B reload
Reload the PAM service and resource-limit policy used for future sessions.
Heartbeat policy is also reloaded and its schedule is reset for existing
sessions.  Existing scoped sessions are reset to the configured process,
memory, and CPU cgroup limit tuple.
.TP
.BI set-session-limits " id"
Set the complete runtime cgroup limit tuple for one managed scoped session.
Zero means unlimited.  Process and memory values accept 0 through 1048576;
CPU percent accepts 0 through 10000.  The session manager may reject sessions
without transient systemd scope ownership.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI --socket " path"
Use an alternate session-manager socket.  The default is
.IR /run/frdp-sesmand/sesmand.sock .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR frdpd (8),
.BR frdp-sesmand (8)
